term got a value), \code{eq(cb, a, b)} and \code{diseq(cb, a, b)} (registered terms
became equal or different), \code{final(cb)} (a full assignment exists; the
last chance to object), \code{created(cb, term)} (a term using a function from
\code{z3_propagator_function()} appeared), \code{decide(cb, term, index, phase)}
(Z3 is about to split on a term) and \code{binding(cb, quantifier, instance)} (Z3 4.15.3; return \code{FALSE} to block a quantifier instance).

Terms are registered before the check with \code{z3_propagate_register()}, or
inside a callback with \code{z3_propagate_register_cb()}. Inside a callback,
\code{cb} is a handle valid only until the callback returns:
\itemize{
\item \code{z3_propagate_consequence(cb, fixed, consequence, lhs, rhs)} states
that \code{consequence} follows from the values of the \code{fixed} terms and the
equalities \code{lhs[[i]] == rhs[[i]]}. With \code{consequence = FALSE} it is a
conflict, and Z3 backtracks.
\item \code{z3_propagate_conflict(cb, fixed)} is the same with \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{z3_next_split(cb, term, index, phase)} suggests the next decision.
}

An error in a callback interrupts the check and is raised as an R error.
Z3 sometimes copies a solver internally; the copy does not run R
callbacks, and z3r warns when that happens.

\code{z3_on_clause(solver, fn)} registers \code{fn(proof_hint, deps, literals)},
called for every clause the solver asserts, infers or deletes (Z3
4.13.0). \code{z3_on_model(optimizer, fn)} registers \code{fn(model)}, called with
each improved model during optimization. \code{z3_set_proof_checker(ctx, fn)}
(Z3 5.3.2) registers \code{fn(proof, assertions)}, called by the SMT-LIB2
interpreter (\code{\link[=z3_eval_smtlib]{z3_eval_smtlib()}}) after unsatisfiable checks when proofs
are enabled; it returns a report string or \code{NULL}. Pass \code{fn = NULL} to
remove it.
